<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Fullscreen must reach the top of the screen on notched Macs.

         Without this key, a `.titled` window entering fullscreen on a display
         with a camera housing is inset BELOW the safe area: macOS hands the
         window `screenHeight - safeAreaInsets.top` (measured on a 16" M4 Pro:
         1290 of 1329 points) and paints the 39pt strip it kept back solid
         black. The window is transparent for the vibrancy backing, so nothing
         of ours covers it — it reads as a dead band across the whole top of
         the app, above the sidebar header AND the work-surface header.

         Setting this zeroes `NSScreen.safeAreaInsets` and drops the auxiliary
         top areas, and the fullscreen window is handed the display's full
         frame. Verified against a standalone AppKit repro of this window's
         exact style mask, in three bundles differing only by this key: unset
         and `false` both letterbox at 1290; `true` fills 1329.

         It is a no-op everywhere else — displays without a camera housing
         already report zero insets, and windowed/zoomed layout is driven by
         `visibleFrame`, which this does not touch. The notch lands over the
         middle of our top strip, which SidebarHeader deliberately leaves as
         empty drag region. -->
    <key>NSPrefersDisplaySafeAreaCompatibilityMode</key>
    <true/>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Aura uses your microphone for huddles — voice chat with teammates working in the same repo.</string>
    <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Aura may use your camera if you choose to share video in a huddle.</string>
    <key>NSScreenCaptureUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Aura uses screen recording to share your screen with teammates in a huddle.</string>
    <key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Aura sends Apple Events to control opening files and folders in your other apps.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
